---
/**
 * /rescue/ — a fixed-price read of software this practice did not build.
 * THE FIFTH PAID THING ON THIS SITE, and the only one whose buyer arrives
 * already convinced they have a problem.
 *
 * IT PASSED THE FOURTH-PRODUCT TEST, WHICH IS NOW THE FIFTH. src/lib/pricing.js
 * states the rule: a new published figure is allowed only for something a
 * visitor could buy without buying any of the others. Nobody here buys a fitted
 * tool, a site, an assessment or a retainer first — they are trying to find out
 * whether they own an asset or a liability. The argument is written out above
 * RESCUE_AUDIT_USD, along with a warning that five is where a price list starts
 * to look like the menu this site refuses, and that the next one should be
 * turned down unless it is as obviously separate as this.
 *
 * THE REFUSAL IS THE PRODUCT AND IT RENDERS HIGH. A read whose author is paid
 * only when it recommends a rebuild cannot credibly say "this is fine for
 * another year" or "this cannot be saved and the money is gone". The fee buys
 * those two sentences. So `alsoSays` sits beside the figure rather than in a
 * reassurance block at the foot of the page — put it at the bottom and it reads
 * as a disclaimer, put it beside the price and it reads as the terms.
 *
 * THE DAY CEILING IS NOT A DETAIL. Reading somebody else's broken software is
 * unbounded work on an input nobody can inspect before quoting, which is exactly
 * why no seller in the spreadsheet-migration market publishes a number. The
 * whole category's answer is to fix the price of the READ and quote the repair
 * after, and the ceiling is what makes that safe for both sides. A reader who
 * meets the fee without it is being sold an open engagement.
 *
 * NO NEW PAPERWORK, DELIBERATELY. A repair that follows is an ordinary build —
 * the statement of work, the 30-day warranty, the 10-business-day nonconformance
 * window and the change order all exist and all already say the right things.
 * The read itself signs nothing, because it has no deliverable to warrant beyond
 * the document it produces.
 *
 * THE COPY NEVER SAYS "AI". Two threads on r/smallbusiness this year asked how
 * to block posts containing the word, and the owner describing automation that
 * actually worked wrote "it doesn't sound like AI to me". This page sells a
 * decision about software somebody already owns. Reaching for the word here
 * would cost the reader and buy nothing.
 *
 * THE META DESCRIPTION CARRIES THE FIGURE, unlike /ai-fit/ and /advisory/. It
 * can: this qualifier's load-bearing half is a day ceiling short enough to sit
 * beside the fee, and "$500, answered inside 2 working days" fits what a search
 * result shows with both halves intact. THIS PARAGRAPH SAID FIVE DAYS AND QUOTED
 * ITS OWN SENTENCE UNTIL 2026-09-11, when the ceiling moved to two -- see
 * RESCUE_AUDIT_DAYS in src/lib/pricing.js for why. Both numbers here derive from
 * that constant at render time, so this comment is the only place a stale figure
 * can survive, which is the class commit 4e756e9 is about.
 */
import MarketingLayout from '../layouts/MarketingLayout.astro';
import { rescueAudit } from '../data/services';
import { ClipboardCheck, Timer, ArrowUpRight } from '@lucide/astro';

const origin = new URL(Astro.site!).origin;

const schema = [
  {
    '@type': 'Service',
    '@id': `${origin}/rescue/#service`,
    name: 'Software rescue audit',
    serviceType: 'Software assessment',
    provider: { '@id': `${origin}/#business` },
    description:
      'A fixed-price written read of software you already have: what is wrong, what repair would cost, and whether repairing beats starting again.',
  },
];
---

<MarketingLayout
  title="You paid for software and it half works"
  description={`A written answer on software you already have — what is wrong, what repair would cost, and whether it is worth repairing. ${rescueAudit.amount}, inside ${rescueAudit.days} working days.`}
  schema={schema}
>
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden border-b border-line">
    <div class="bg-grid bg-grid-fade pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 -z-10 opacity-60"></div>
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 pt-16 pb-10 sm:pt-24 sm:pb-14">
      <p class="mono-label">A read of what you have · in writing · inside a week</p>
      <h1 class="mt-3 max-w-3xl font-display text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-5xl">
        You paid for software and it half works.
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        {rescueAudit.whoItIsFor}
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        You have probably been told it just needs a bit more work. That may be true. It may also be
        the most expensive sentence anyone has said to you this year, and nobody who has said it so
        far was being paid to find out.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-8 max-w-2xl">
        <div class="panel flex flex-col gap-2 p-6">
          <p class="font-display text-xl font-semibold leading-snug text-ink">{rescueAudit.line}</p>
          <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">{rescueAudit.qualifier}</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      {/* THE REFUSAL RENDERS BESIDE THE FIGURE, NOT AT THE FOOT OF THE PAGE.
          At the bottom it reads as a disclaimer; here it reads as the terms, and
          it is the actual reason the read costs anything at all. */}
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-3xl leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">{rescueAudit.alsoSays}</p>

      <ul class="mt-7 grid max-w-3xl gap-2">
        {rescueAudit.includes.map((item) => (
          <li class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-faint">— {item}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>

      <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
        <a class="btn-primary" href="/contact/?intent=consult">
          Tell me what you have <ArrowUpRight class="size-4" />
        </a>
        <a class="link-sweep text-sm" href="/services/">Or see what I build from scratch →</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-6 py-14 sm:py-20">
    <div class="grid gap-6 sm:grid-cols-2">
      <div class="panel flex flex-col gap-3 p-7">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <Timer class="size-5 text-accent" />
          <span class="mono-label">The limit</span>
        </div>
        <p class="font-display text-lg font-semibold leading-snug text-ink">{rescueAudit.days} working days, and then it is written.</p>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">{rescueAudit.ceiling}</p>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-faint">
          That bound is what makes a fixed price honest on something neither of us can size in
          advance. Without it you would be buying an open meter, and I would be guessing.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="panel flex flex-col gap-3 p-7">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <ClipboardCheck class="size-5 text-accent" />
          <span class="mono-label">What happens after</span>
        </div>
        <p class="font-display text-lg font-semibold leading-snug text-ink">Repair is a separate decision, and yours.</p>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
          If the answer says repair is worth it, that gets scoped and quoted on its own — by me, or
          by anybody else you like. The document is written so another developer can work from it.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-faint">
          Nothing is signed for the read itself. There is no deliverable to warrant beyond the
          answer, and you are not committing to anything past it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 pb-14 sm:pb-20">
    <div class="panel p-8">
      <p class="mono-label">Why pay for this at all</p>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        Because the two most valuable answers cost me money to give. "This is fine, leave it alone
        for another year" ends the conversation. "This cannot be saved" ends it faster. Somebody
        reading your software for free has every reason to find work in it, and you would have no way
        of telling that apart from a real finding.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        The fee is small on purpose. It is not the engagement — it is the thing that lets the answer
        be true.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</MarketingLayout>
